A||||9||Yellow85||Please!! I need help!!||||1||
Z||000000||Yellow85||11-29-1999||01:05 AM||||Hi all,<P>My meter panel light and light at center console dosen't turn on when i turn on switch.  Every thing like tail light and things are working, but not panel lights.<BR>I don't know much about electric so please help me with detail if you know how to solve this problem.  Thank you!!<P>I almost got speeding ticket few days ago because i coudn't see my speed meter at night time(i don't remember how many times i thank officer).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick||11-29-1999||01:48 AM||||<BR>Well, to start with, you haven't got the <B>dimmer</B> for the dash lights turned down or off do you?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Yellow85||11-29-1999||02:52 AM||||To Patrick<P>No, dimmer is all way up and i also checked fuse too.  Any other guess?  Thanks!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||dogtired||11-29-1999||10:11 AM||||Ill take a shot but not sure.<BR>Under your dash on the right side of the steering colum (you have to stand on your head to get to it) is what I call a heat-sink. It is alunimum color piece if metal about 2 1/2 square with a couple wires going to it. In the center of it is a electronic device about the size of a quarter US 25cents (electrode/resistor/transistor) I understand this is part of your dash light dimmer system. It is held up there with 2 screws. It should have a coating of white grease between the electronic device and the heat sink and after age the grease will dryup. You might try re-greasing it or get a replacement from a parts car?<BR>How is that for telling you something more than I know about?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Anyway when I was having all my elect problems I was told to look there. It didn't fix my problem (mine was head and tail lights) but this may help you.||||reg||5
Z||000004||TOM||11-29-1999||11:31 AM||||Could also be a burned out dimmer switch. They do get bad.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Yellow85||11-29-1999||05:30 PM||||Thank you Dogtired & Tom!!  I will check both parts.  If it doesn't work, i will ask you guys for more help.  Again, thanks a lot!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||RFMike||11-29-1999||07:36 PM||||Pull the switch housing and check to see if you have power to the switch.  If you do, replace the switch.  If you don't, work back down the circuit until you find power, then locate the device or location of the problem.<BR>Good luck,<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||11-30-1999||12:39 PM||||Had a similar situation with a Firebird that I used to own. Was able to remove the whole heatsink from under the dash. Don't know if you can do that with the Fiero, or not.<BR>The metal thing on the heat sink, that dogtired referred to, is a transistor. The dimmer knob varies the voltage to the transistor (low current), and the transistor varies the voltage to the dash lights (higher current). There are two prongs that stick out of the back side of the transistor, through the heatsink, and into two metal sockets. Those two metal sockets can get loose, and won't grip the prongs. You can bend the prongs slightly, or solder the prongs into the sockets. If you take the transistor off to do this, be sure to reinstall the clear insulator. The white grease is thermal compound. It is used to wick the heat out of the transistor and into the heatsink. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||11-30-1999||07:53 PM||||if you disassmble the dimmer, replace the heat sink compound between the transitor and the heat sink. put a dot on each side of any insulator. heat sink compound is available at radio shack or most other electonic suply shops.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Yellow85||12-01-1999||12:44 AM||||Hi all, <P>I just found cause of problem.  It was one of those line for light bulb behinde dash(i mean those lines that cover by plastic sheet kind thing).  One of them were short so ground wasn't there.<BR>Anyway, thanks for help guys!!||||reg||1
